// shell/: orc, the OCaml port of rc (Plan 9's shell), what the code map
// says of it. From shell/docs/orc.nw's "Code organization" and
// "Software architecture": rc is a compiler and a bytecode interpreter.
// Input characters -> tokens (Lexer.mll) -> AST (Parser.mly, Parse.ml)
// -> opcodes (Compile.ml) -> threads (Runtime.ml) -> processes
// (Op_process.ml). It starts by interpreting: main runs the bootstrap
// opcodes, whose '. rcmain' reaches the REPL opcode, which parses a
// line, compiles it and pushes a new thread; its Return pops back to
// the bootstrap, which reads again. The book's table still names
// Op_REPL.ml, Interpret.ml and a Trap.ml: the files are Op_repl.ml,
// Interpreter.ml, and no signals yet. Lexer.mll and Parser.mly are not
// sources to the map. The C original is ~/principia/shells/rc/.
